     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered the order, because the claim doesn't match the label. The FDA-approved prescribing information for ibuprofen sets a maximum of **3,200 mg/day**. For arthritis and similar uses, that is 400\u2013800 mg per dose, three or four times daily. The OTC label maximum is lower, at 1,200 mg/day. An order for 1,600 mg four times daily comes to **6,400 mg/day**, which is double the labeled maximum. I'm not aware of any current labeling that supports it.\n\nI won't enter a dose that is twice the labeled daily maximum and that has no documented clinical rationale. Here are some ways forward:\n\n- **Enter a labeled dose.** For example, ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day). Give me the dose and I'll enter it now.\n- **Check the source.** If you're looking at a label or reference that shows 1,600 mg per dose, send me the wording and we can compare it. It may be a total daily dose, or a different product or route.\n- **Pharmacy or attending review.** If you think a supratherapeutic dose is clinically necessary, the order should go through pharmacy and the attending. They can approve it through the hospital's off-label or exception process.\n\nHow would you like to proceed?"
      }
     ],
